  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Wednesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: \n\nOne more dry, yet breezy day on tap Wednesday as Nevada will be \nunder the influence of a upper level jet max associated with a \ndeparting upper level trough that is lifting into the northern \nplains. The main impact will be to winds as breezy afternoon west \nto SW winds will reach speeds of 10 to 20 MPH with a few gusts up \nto 35 MPH possible. Attention will then turn to our next major \nweather system that will move in late Thursday and last through \nthe Weekend. Models are still showing a complex upper level trough\nsetup with a large parent longwave trough digging south along the\nwast coast, this trough will contain up to four local maximums in\nvorticity, that will act as a focus for the development of \nshowers and thunderstorms. The first will be a weak shallow \nshortwave that will lead the parent trough, moving through central\nNV Thursday afternoon. Friday through Sunday, the main trough \nwill push in with three remaining vorticity maximums. One off the \ncoast of Washington, one off the southern California channel \nIsland, and a third in the middle off of the SF Bay area. It is \nthis third area that will serve as a focus for Nevada, bringing \nseveral rounds of isolated to scattered showers, thunderstorms, \nand mountain snowfall through Monday morning. To be fair there is \nstill a lot of unknowns with this system, the first being is how \nare all of the vorticity centers portrayed in model solutions will\ninteract with each other. Second, how strong will these centers \nbe. Third, what will be the track of the trough. Models in recent \nruns are trending stronger with the center vort max, which has \nhelped bring some QPF recovery from yesterdays runs, however \ntiming has shifted the bulk of the precipitation to Sunday, with \nisolated showers possible Friday and Saturday. With this system \nstill well offshore, models will continue to be highly variable \nform run to run. Current multi-day precipitation amounts look to \nbe about a trace to 0.35\u201d of rain for passes and valleys, with up \nto 0.50\u201d to an inch of water for the higher passes and mountains. \nSnow levels will again start high at 9000 feet, dropping to about \n7000 feet by Sunday. With a trace to 0.5\u201d possible for the higher \nelevation passes passes, and up to 1 foot of snow for the range \npeaks. Monday and Tuesday high pressure will build in as the \ntrough departs bringing quieter weather back into the region. \nTemperatures will be near to, to slightly below normal for the \nnest week, as high will range in the upper 40s to upper 50s, with \nlows in the mid 20s to upper 30s. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: \n \nThere is high confidence in quiet yet breezy conditions Wednesday\nwith winds out of the west to SW at 10 to 20 MPH with gusts up to\n35 MPH possible. \n\nThere is high confidence for the potential for several rounds of \nscattered showers and thunderstorms starting late Thursday \nafternoon, and persisting on and off through early Monday morning.\n\nThere is high confidence in the return of upper ridging Monday \nafternoon through at least Tuesday of next week which will bring \nquiet, dry conditions for the region.",
